<section class="panel">
	<div class="hero">
		<div class="left">
			<div class="eyebrow">postmarketOS &middot; Plasma Mobile &middot; aarch64</div>
			<h1>A POCO X3 that works like a phone.</h1>
			<p class="lede">A signed apk repository with a patched kernel, ten forked
			packages and six applications, for two Xiaomi devices running postmarketOS
			edge. Install it on what you already have, or flash a ready image.</p>
			<div class="btns">
				<a class="btn" href="install.html">Install it</a>
				<a class="btn ghost" href="devices.html">What does not work</a>
			</div>
			<div class="grid tight">
				<div><div class="num">2</div><small>devices</small></div>
				<div><div class="num">78</div><small>kernel patches</small></div>
				<div><div class="num">69</div><small>packages</small></div>
				<div><div class="num">6</div><small>applications</small></div>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div class="right">
			<div><b>Repository</b>@URL@/main</div>
			<div><b>Signing key</b>@KEY@</div>
			<div><b>Base</b>postmarketOS edge, Plasma Mobile, systemd</div>
			<div><b>Devices</b>xiaomi-surya (POCO X3 NFC) &middot; xiaomi-vayu (POCO X3 Pro, experimental)</div>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>

<section>
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="h"><span>01</span><h2>Quickstart</h2></div>
		<p>Three commands on a postmarketOS install that already boots. This is Alpine,
		so it is <b>apk</b>, not apt.</p>
		<div class="grid">
			<div>
				<div class="cap">STEP 1</div>
				<div class="t">Trust the key</div>
				<p>Packages are signed. Without the key apk refuses them, and it is right to.</p>
				<div class="sh" style="margin-top:14px"><pre>sudo wget -qO /etc/apk/keys/@KEY@ \
  @URL@/keys/@KEY@</pre></div>
			</div>
			<div>
				<div class="cap">STEP 2</div>
				<div class="t">Add the repository</div>
				<p>Untagged, on purpose: a tagged line makes apk prefer upstream for every
				dependency, quietly.</p>
				<div class="sh" style="margin-top:14px"><pre>echo @URL@/main | \
  sudo tee -a /etc/apk/repositories
sudo apk update</pre></div>
			</div>
			<div>
				<div class="cap">STEP 3</div>
				<div class="t">Install</div>
				<p>The kernel comes with it. From here on <code>apk upgrade</code> keeps
				everything current.</p>
				<div class="sh" style="margin-top:14px"><pre>sudo apk add utsugi-surya-full</pre></div>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div class="note" style="margin-top:16px"><i></i><div>
			<b>Reboot as a separate, deliberate step.</b>
			On the surya a kernel that does not boot is not a failed install, it is a reboot
			loop: the watchdog fires 20&nbsp;s after PID&nbsp;1 and there is no screen left to fix
			it from. <a href="install.html">Read the installation page first.</a>
		</div></div>
	</div>
</section>

<section class="panel">
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="h"><span>02</span><h2>What this is</h2></div>
		<p>Everything that makes these phones work, packaged so it installs and updates
		like software instead of like an afternoon. It used to be an installer script
		copying files over ssh; now it is a repository with a signed index.</p>
		<div class="grid quiet">
			<div>
				<div class="t">Add it to what you have</div>
				<p>A postmarketOS install that boots gets the kernel, the fixes and the
				applications, and tracks them with <code>apk upgrade</code>.</p>
			</div>
			<div>
				<div class="t">Or flash a whole image</div>
				<p>Built from exactly what is published, with the repository line and the
				key already inside. First boot is already current.</p>
			</div>
			<div>
				<div class="t">Encrypted, built by you</div>
				<p>There is no encrypted image to download, and that is deliberate: the
				master key would be born on the machine that built it.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div class="next">
			<a href="devices.html"><b>First</b><span>What does not work</span></a>
			<a href="features.html"><b>Then</b><span>What you get</span></a>
			<a href="install.html"><b>When ready</b><span>Installation</span></a>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>
